{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790517550,"nonce":"9mhNrqcGvvpozGwFTdfSrC+zRHw=","title":"wasm threads: a world stop parks agents inside runtime code","message":"With collections deferred to compiled polls on wasm (501f8a5), a single\nagent is safe: it collects only with compiled frames below. Other agents are\nnot. The collector stops the world, and every other agent parks wherever it\nreaches a safepoint -- including inside runtime code, e.g. the GC-lock slow\npath in an allocation (`GC_LOCK.wake_for_world_stop`), with pointers in wasm\nlocals no scan reads.\n\nReproducer: examples/browser/demo/Threads.hx built with\n`ASH_WASM_FIBERS=1 ash --build t.wasm --target wasm32-wasip1-threads`, run\nwith `ASH_GC_STRESS=1 ash run t.wasm`. The four-threads-allocating part\nthrows its haxe.ValueException (answers differ from the single-thread run)\nand the agent aborts (\"hlp_throw called without active trap\"). Without\nstress it passes; a compute-only four-thread program passes under stress.\n\nFix direction: on wasm, park another agent only at a compiled poll (callout\ndepth \u003c= 0) or at a blocking point that holds nothing unrooted; an agent in\nruntime code finishes to its next such point before it counts as stopped.\nApplies to caribou's heap the same way (caribou e964da6).","files":null}]}